import LeaseProtocol from '@/components/LeaseProtocol.astro'
import { exampleLanguages } from '@/lib/snippets'

const langs = exampleLanguages('workflow')
---

<Doc
  title="Workers"
  description="How a worker serves the steps of a durable run: registering definitions, claiming leases, heartbeats, complete and fail, the four 409 reasons, at_most_once, scaling out, and the worker key scope."
  lede="A worker is your own process, serving step bodies to the daemon. This page is the protocol it speaks, and why each call is there."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>What a worker is</h2>
  <p>
    A worker holds the bodies of the claimable steps and asks the daemon for
    work. It runs where your code runs, with your imports, your connection pool
    and your credentials, and the daemon never sees any of them. The daemon
    hands it a lease with the rendered input and the prior steps; the worker
    runs the body and reports how it went. That is the whole relationship.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every SDK ships one. It registers the workflows it holds, long-polls for
    leases, heartbeats while a body runs, and answers every refusal by its
    reason. The example below is the same program as the one on the
    <a href="/docs/workflows" class="link">Workflows</a> page; this is the part
    that serves it.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" example="workflow" region="worker" only={langs} title="serve the bodies" />

  <h2>The protocol</h2>
  <p>
    Five calls, and a worker that speaks them can be written against the HTTP
    API without an SDK. Every SDK's worker is a client of these and nothing
    more.
  </p>

  <LeaseProtocol class="not-prose my-8" />

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Call</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>POST /v1/api/workers/register</code></td>
        <td>Announces the worker and stores the definitions it holds. The answer carries <code>lease_ttl_ms</code> and <code>heartbeat_ms</code>, the two numbers the rest of the protocol runs on.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>POST /v1/api/steps/claim</code></td>
        <td>Offers up to <code>max</code> runnable steps, holding the request open for up to <code>wait_ms</code> when there are none. The daemon holds a claim for at most four fifths of its request timeout, 24 seconds at the default, and answers at once when it stops, so a claim can come back empty before <code>wait_ms</code>: claim again. A claim may be limited to one run, which is how a script drives its own run to the end.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>POST /v1/api/steps/{'{lease}'}/started</code></td>
        <td>Declares that the body is running. Before the body, always: it is what tells the daemon the difference between offered and running.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>POST /v1/api/steps/{'{lease}'}/heartbeat</code></td>
        <td>Extends the lease. The only evidence the daemon has that a worker is alive.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>POST /v1/api/steps/{'{lease}'}/complete</code>, <code>/fail</code></td>
        <td>Finishes the step: the output, a patch to the run's state and the usage the daemon prices; or an error with the class that decides whether it is retried.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Leases</h2>
  <p>
    A lease is one attempt, held by one worker, for a bounded time. It carries
    the rendered input, the prior steps keyed by id, the run's state and
    correlation, a key that is stable across retries of this step, and the
    trace and span ids of the step span the daemon opened. A body that records
    its own spans through the step context puts them under that span, so the
    model call inside a step lands in the run's trace at the right depth.
  </p>
  <p>
    A lease the worker stops heartbeating expires. The daemon marks the attempt
    orphaned and, after a reclaim window, requeues it under
    <code>at_least_once</code> or fails it under <code>at_most_once</code>. A
    worker that comes back from a pause and posts against a reclaimed lease is
    not ignored: it is told.
  </p>

  <h2>The four refusals</h2>
  <p>
    A worker call the daemon cannot accept is refused with a 409 carrying one
    of four reasons. They are closed, and every SDK's worker handles each one
    by name, because a worker that collapses them into one branch abandons
    steps it has already finished or reports a failure for a success.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Reason</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What the worker does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>already_applied</code></td>
        <td>This lease's own earlier complete or fail landed and its reply was lost.</td>
        <td>Treats it as success and posts nothing more.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>fenced</code></td>
        <td>A later attempt exists, or another lease ended this one.</td>
        <td>Abandons the work and posts nothing more for this lease. A fenced heartbeat also cancels the running body through its context.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>run_terminal</code></td>
        <td>The run has ended, by finishing, failing, timing out or being cancelled.</td>
        <td>Stops, and stops claiming for that run.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>not_started</code></td>
        <td>A complete or fail arrived for an <code>at_most_once</code> attempt whose <code>started</code> never did.</td>
        <td>Reports it loudly as a bug in the worker. The SDK workers cannot produce it; a hand-written one can.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The body</h2>
  <p>
    A body receives a context, its rendered input and the prior steps. The
    context is where it reports what the daemon should keep: a patch to the
    run's state, merged on <code>complete</code> with top-level keys replacing;
    the tokens it spent, which the daemon prices with the same book it prices
    spans with; and a cancellation signal that fires when the lease is lost or
    the run ends, so a body that hands it to its own I/O stops when it should.
    The tokens add themselves up: a span the body records through the context,
    and every span under it, adds its usage to the step's when it ends, so a
    body that records its model calls as spans never sums by hand, and
    <code>set_usage</code> is the explicit override for one that records none.
  </p>
  <p>
    A body ends by returning its output or by raising. Raise the SDK's
    retryable error to be retried within the step's budget; raise a typed step
    failure to name any other class; anything else is fatal. The message is
    sent to the daemon and shown in the journal. A stack trace is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    A lease for a definition, a version or a step the worker does not hold is
    failed <code>unservable</code> and the body never runs. The attempt goes
    back to the queue for a worker that does hold it, without spending the
    retry budget, which is what lets two versions of a worker run side by side
    through a deploy.
  </p>

  <h2>Scaling out</h2>
  <p>
    Run as many workers as you like against one daemon. Each claims up to its
    own concurrency, the daemon hands every attempt to exactly one of them,
    and the fence makes a lease that two workers somehow both hold resolve to
    one winner. A worker's id is recorded on every attempt it took, so the
    journal says which process ran a step. Give each process a name, or let
    the SDK derive one from the host.
  </p>
  <p>
    A claim is a long poll, so an idle fleet costs the daemon one open request
    per worker and no traffic. While it waits, a claim looks for work when a
    run starts, a step ends, a deadline fires or an event resumes a wait, and
    at most twice a second otherwise. A busy one is bounded by the workers' own
    concurrency, and a body that takes an hour costs one heartbeat every ten
    seconds and nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2>Keys</h2>
  <p>
    The worker calls take the <code>worker</code> scope; starting, signalling
    and cancelling runs take <code>execute</code>. Both fall back to the write
    keys when they are unset, and an admin key holds both. A worker that only
    serves steps needs no key that can start a run, and a webhook that only
    publishes events needs no key that can claim one. See
    <a href="/docs/configuration" class="link">Configuration</a> for the flags.
  </p>
  <p>
    The worker calls are not recorded in the audit log, although a lease
    carries the step's input and the prior steps' outputs. They are a machine
    loop that claims many times a minute, and a row per claim would bury the
    reads a person made. Reading a run back through the run endpoints is
    recorded; see <a href="/docs/security" class="link">Security</a>.
  </p>

